  "progressionNotes": "Take every set to absolute failure and use rest-pause and partials on all exercises.",
  "guide": [
    {
      "heading": "What the HIT block is for",
      "body": [
        "The HIT block is a desensitizing period after the volume progression that built up through your split, and a great way to stimulate your muscles in a different manner. It is not for beginners.",
        "The working sets of the block should be half of your MAV (the working sets in week 5 of the split you just ran)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to run it",
      "body": [
        "Take every set to absolute failure. To choose the right weight for the few sets you have, do 3 warm-up sets first.",
        "Rest-pause and partials are better than assisted reps, so use them on all exercises.",
        "Your week runs Legs, Push, Pull, Legs, Push, Pull. Arms is only programmed in week 4.",
        "The app assigns sessions to your training days in order, so the week counter follows sessions rather than the calendar."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Warm-up sets",
      "body": [
        "Do 3 warm-up sets: one with the bar, one with 50%, and the last with 75% of the weight you plan on hitting on your first working set."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Training techniques used",
      "body": [
        "RP = Rest-pause: after you reach technical failure, wait 10-15 seconds and squeeze out some more reps.",
        "P = Long-length partials: partial reps with the target muscle at its lengthened position, at the end of a movement such as the bottom of a curl, the top of a pulldown or the bottom of a bench press.",
        "S = Intra-set stretch: after you finish your set, stretch that muscle for 35 seconds.",
        "Superset: do the two exercises back to back, then rest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Terms",
      "body": [
        "RIR = reps in reserve. If a set says 3 RIR, pick a weight and stop at a rep where you feel you could still push out 3 more reps.",
        "%1RM = percentage of your one-rep max. For 80%, take 80% of your one-rep max and use that weight."
      ]
    }
  ],
